# What must never enter the Docker build context.
#
# The context is uploaded to the daemon before a single instruction runs, so an
# unfiltered context here would ship 694 MB of .venv, 131 MB of node_modules and
# a 43 MB uncompressed benchmark log across a socket on every build (measured on
# 2026-09-03 with `du -sh`; the whole tree is 972 MB). Everything
# excluded below is either regenerable inside the image, host-specific, or
# already present in a form the Dockerfile prefers.
#
# This file is a deny list rather than the "ignore everything, then re-include"
# idiom. A deny list is auditable: each line says what is being kept out and
# why, and a new source directory is included by default instead of silently
# vanishing from the image because nobody remembered to re-include it.
#
# ONE RULE THAT IS NOT THE .gitignore RULE, and it is the easiest thing in this
# file to get wrong: a .dockerignore pattern is matched against the whole path
# from the context root, so a bare `__pycache__/` excludes only a directory of
# that name at the top level and leaves every nested one in the context. Git
# treats the same line as "anywhere in the tree". Every pattern below that is
# meant to apply at any depth therefore carries an explicit `**/`; measured over
# this tree, forgetting it left 97 stale `.pyc` files (and 3 MB) in the context.

# --- Host-specific build products -----------------------------------------
# .venv is a macOS/Linux-host virtualenv with absolute paths baked into its
# scripts and platform-specific wheels; the image builds its own.
.venv/
# node_modules is reinstalled from package-lock.json by `npm ci` in the
# frontend stage, so that the image's dependency tree is the locked one rather
# than whatever the developer's tree has drifted to.
frontend/node_modules/
# frontend/dist is rebuilt in the frontend stage. Copying the host's build
# would make the image's UI depend on when someone last ran `npm run build`.
frontend/dist/
**/*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# --- Caches ----------------------------------------------------------------
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.vite/
# The osmnx HTTP cache from `qroute osm fetch`; 15 MB of downloaded Overpass
# responses that only speed up a re-fetch on the machine that made them.
data/cache/

# --- Large regenerable data ------------------------------------------------
# The road graphs are ~40 MB and are deliberately NOT baked into the image;
# see docs/deployment.md for the reasoning and for how they are supplied at
# run time. data/osm/networks.json and index.json are small and are kept.
data/osm/*.graphml
# The definitive benchmark's raw log is 43 MB uncompressed and 4.9 MB gzipped.
# The Dockerfile copies the .gz and expands it inside the image, so excluding
# the expanded file here saves 43 MB of context upload without losing anything.
results/runs/*/rows.jsonl

# --- Version control and tooling -------------------------------------------
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.dockerignore
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
.idea/
.vscode/
**/.cache/
**/.DS_Store
**/*.log
**/*.prof

# --- Not needed at run time -------------------------------------------------
# The test suite runs in CI and in the developer's checkout, not in the
# production image. Keeping it out avoids shipping pytest fixtures and keeps
# the image's contents equal to what the service actually executes.
tests/
docs/
